# Job + exporter signal lifetime

`Job` is a non-QObject owned by `unique_ptr`. `QPointer<Job>` will not compile. Do not re-identify the job with `jobById(id)` inside exporter lambdas: a queued `progressChanged` / `exportFinished` can run after `dismissJob` or during manager teardown.

- Capture the `Job*` (heap address is stable after `jobs_.push_back`).
- Pass the **exporter** as the `connect` context so Qt drops the slot on `disconnect()` / `deleteLater()`.
- Guard with `job->state == Exporting`. Make `finishJob` a no-op if already terminal.
- `discardExporter()` must `release()`, `disconnect()`, `cancelExport()`, `deleteLater()`. Call it from `finishJob` **and** the manager destructor. Do not leave `unique_ptr<ClipExporter>` connected until member destruction (`~QObject` runs after `jobs_`).
- Never `erase` a Job while `exporter` is non-null. `Job` outlives a connected exporter.
- Do not make `Job` a `QObject` just for `QPointer`; keep it a plain struct (`qt-raii.mdc`).

```cpp
// BAD — id lookup; slot lives on the manager after the Job is gone
connect(exporter, &ClipExporter::progressChanged, this, [this, jobId](int current, int total) {
  Job* job = jobById(jobId);
  if (!job) return;
  job->currentClip = current;
});

// GOOD — exporter context dies with the worker; Job* is valid until then
connect(exporter, &ClipExporter::progressChanged, exporter,
        [this, jobPointer](int current, int total) {
  if (jobPointer->state != JobState::Exporting) return;
  jobPointer->currentClip = current;
  jobPointer->totalClips = total;
});
```
